$10$ points lie in a plane.
Exactly $4$ of these points lie on a single straight line, but no other three points are collinear.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0.2,0.8) -- (5.4,0.8);
\fill (0.8,0.8) circle (2pt) node[below] {$A$};
\fill (2.1,0.8) circle (2pt) node[below] {$B$};
\fill (3.5,0.8) circle (2pt) node[below] {$C$};
\fill (4.8,0.8) circle (2pt) node[below] {$D$};
\fill (0.7,3.1) circle (2pt) node[left] {$E$};
\fill (1.3,4.1) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$F$};
\fill (2.3,4.4) circle (2pt) node[above] {$G$};
\fill (3.6,2.2) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$H$};
\fill (4.9,2.2) circle (2pt) node[right] {$I$};
\fill (4.5,4.3) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$J$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
How many distinct triangles can be formed with vertices at these points?

$11$ points are chosen in a plane.
Exactly $5$ points are collinear and no other $3$ points are collinear.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0.2,0.75) -- (5.0,4.35);
\fill (0.8,1.2) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$A$};
\fill (1.7,1.875) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$B$};
\fill (2.6,2.55) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$C$};
\fill (3.5,3.225) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$D$};
\fill (4.4,3.9) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$E$};
\fill (4.9,0.9) circle (2pt) node[right] {$F$};
\fill (3.4,4.7) circle (2pt) node[above] {$G$};
\fill (4.9,2.4) circle (2pt) node[right] {$H$};
\fill (2.0,4.9) circle (2pt) node[above] {$I$};
\fill (4.0,1.8) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$J$};
\fill (1.3,3.9) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$K$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
How many straight lines can be drawn by connecting pairs of these points?
